{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Root cause NOT proven — ticket stays OPEN. Mechanism proven, runner trigger not.\n\nPer the \"no guess-patch\" rule this is evidence, not a fix. Two hypotheses in the original\nwrite-up are **refuted**, one new latent bug is **proven**, and the remaining candidate is\nstrongly corroborated but unreproduced.\n\n### The exact invocation\n\n`src/rebar/_store/s3_doctor.py:248`, in `_publish_and_prune`:\n\n    create = _git(base_path, \"bundle\", \"create\", bundle_path, \"HEAD\", f\"refs/heads/{ref}\")\n\n`_git` -> `gitutil.run_git` -> plain `subprocess.run`. **No retry wrapper is in this path at\nall** — not `_with_index_lock_retry`, not `_with_transient_head_retry`, not the per-store\nadvisory git-op lock. The whole `s3_doctor` module sits outside rebar's self-healing git seam.\n\n### What `fatal: bad object HEAD` actually means (proven by experiment)\n\n| repo state | git's message |\n|---|---|\n| unborn HEAD | `fatal: ambiguous argument 'HEAD': unknown revision …` |\n| HEAD symref -> missing branch | `fatal: ambiguous argument 'HEAD': …` |\n| HEAD resolves, **loose object removed** | `fatal: bad object HEAD` <- exact CI match, single line |\n| object file present but empty | `error: object file … is empty` x3, then `fatal: bad object HEAD` |\n| object corrupt | `error: inflate: data stream error` / `fatal: loose object … is corrupt` |\n\nSo `bad object` is git's `get_reference()` path: the name resolved to an OID but\n`parse_object()` returned NULL. It means **the object was unreadable**, not that a rev was\nunresolvable.\n\n- **REFUTED: \"the literal `HEAD` argument failed to resolve.\"** An unborn or dangling HEAD\n  produces `ambiguous argument`, never `bad object`. Also, `HEAD` is merely the FIRST rev\n  argument — with the object missing, bundling `refs/heads/tickets` alone dies with\n  `fatal: bad object refs/heads/tickets`. The word HEAD in the message carries no information.\n- **REFUTED: \"`b41953…` in the message proves the object existed.\"** `git rev-parse HEAD`\n  prints the sha by reading the ref file even when the object is absent, so `_fold_heads`'\n  `rev-parse` would have returned it either way.\n- **PROVEN: the object was ABSENT, not empty or corrupt.** The empty and corrupt cases emit\n  extra `error:` lines first; the CI stderr was the single `fatal:` line, and the full job log\n  greps clean for `object file … is empty`, `is corrupt`, `unable to unpack`, `inflate`.\n\n### Mechanism reproduction\n\nRunning the real `heal_multi_bundle` against the real harness with `s3_doctor._git` wrapped to\ndelete the merge commit's loose object immediately before the bundle call:\n\n    could not bundle merged tip ad862009255c3e329ae103e80fd2430ac6bbe95a for ref tickets: fatal: bad object HEAD\n\nByte-for-byte the CI shape. This proves the mechanism (object absent from the ODB at bundle\ntime). It does **not** prove what made it absent on the runner.\n\n### What is ruled out as the trigger\n\n- **Background auto-gc/repack.** Instrumented a real heal run: the healed tracker has **2 loose\n  objects and 1 pack**, against `gc.auto` 6700 and `gc.autoPackLimit` 50. Auto-gc cannot fire.\n  (Worth noting separately: this ad-hoc test clone has `gc.autoDetach` at its default `true`,\n  because rebar's `init._gc_config_unit` ensure — the ADR 0051 / bug 88eb remediation forcing\n  foreground gc — never runs on a test-created clone. Not the trigger here, but the protection\n  rebar built for its own stores is absent in these fixtures.)\n- **Leaked `GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY` / `GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES`.** No test writes git env\n  vars into `os.environ`; a leak would also have applied to the merge, not just the bundle.\n- **Cross-test interference.** Every scenario builder uses its own `tmp_path`. The failing test\n  is functionally identical to its four passing siblings apart from a pass-through `write_lock`\n  spy — which itself argues the flake could have landed on any of them.\n- **Local reproduction: 0 failures in 27 runs** (15 serial with `-p no:randomly`, 12 under\n  `-n 4`).\n\n### Remaining candidate (suspected, unproven)\n\nA runner-side transient object-DB availability blip. Corroborated by three documented instances\nof the same class in this repo, all on the same runner fleet:\n- `gitutil.py` `_TRANSIENT_HEAD_MARKERS` — `could not parse HEAD`, bug childsafe-special-springtail,\n  \"a runner-FS read hiccup, NOT data corruption… the identical invocation succeeds on retry\".\n- `event_append.py` `_TRANSIENT_ADD_MARKERS` — the write-side object-DB transient.\n- `event_append.py` `_INVALID_OBJECT_MARKERS`, bug 4c1c — \"a loose object that vanished between\n  `git add` and `git commit` — a background gc repack, **or a partial object write under FS/memory\n  pressure**\". That is literally this vanished-object case, already observed in production here.\n\nStrong prior art is not proof, so no retry is being added on this evidence.\n\n### If this recurs, the next step is bounded and specific\n\n1. Route `s3_doctor._git` through the existing `gitutil` self-healing seam instead of raw\n   `run_git`, so it inherits the lock retry and transient-marker family rather than growing a\n   fourth private copy. `git bundle create` mutates no store state — it reads objects and writes\n   a scratch file the caller re-creates and deletes — so it satisfies the same idempotency\n   precondition `_with_transient_head_retry` documents.\n2. If a marker is added, scope it to the missing-object family and distinguish it in the raised\n   error: `S3DoctorConflict(... \"heads conflict; run rebar fsck-recover\")` misdescribes an ODB\n   read failure, and its hint is the wrong advice for a merge that never conflicted.\n\n### Separately PROVEN latent bug (not this failure) — filed as its own ticket\n\nWhen `current_branch != ref` — a detached HEAD, a differently-checked-out branch, or a\nbare/mirror tracker — line 248 still bundles the literal `HEAD`, which then names an unrelated\ncommit (silently bundling the wrong tip) or dies `ambiguous argument 'HEAD'` on an unborn HEAD.\nThe `HEAD` argument IS load-bearing: a bundle containing only `refs/heads/tickets` clones with\n`warning: remote HEAD refers to nonexistent ref, unable to checkout` and no commits, which would\nbreak `s3_doctor_harness.healed_tip`. So the fix is to make HEAD explicit and correct, not to\ndrop it.\n\n### On the \"shared trigger with bug ac76\" note\n\nBoth did fail in run 31349549323, but in **different jobs** (this one in the py3.11 integration\nphase, ac76 in the py3.13 unit phase). ac76 has since been root-caused to a shared-index commit\nrace entirely inside its own test, with no runner-resource component. So the two are NOT one\nmechanism. The only shared factor is that a loaded xdist runner widens pre-existing windows."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786502261902111001,"uuid":"1e11d341-975a-477a-814f-b59c12c5a25a"}